WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.140
Scrape your knee, and new skin often covers the spot in about 1 to 2 weeks.

2
00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:11.320
Where did those new skin cells come from?

3
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.200
Zoom in on the edge of the scrape.

4
00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.800
Skin cells near the gap divide, and each one becomes two.

5
00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:22.200
Some cells also crawl into the gap.

6
00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:26.200
Every new cell comes from a cell that was already there.

7
00:00:27.233 --> 00:00:29.013
Here is the harder question.

8
00:00:30.453 --> 00:00:32.593
Most cells carry a full set of instructions.

9
00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:37.753
When one cell becomes two, how does each new cell get a complete set?

10
00:00:38.833 --> 00:00:40.873
The instructions sit on chromosomes.

11
00:00:42.353 --> 00:00:46.333
These are threads inside the nucleus that carry the cell's instructions.

12
00:00:47.733 --> 00:00:51.333
Our model cell has four of them: red, blue, green and yellow.

13
00:00:52.467 --> 00:00:54.987
A dividing cell goes through the cell cycle.

14
00:00:56.147 --> 00:00:57.507
It has two main parts.

15
00:00:58.767 --> 00:00:59.987
Interphase comes first.

16
00:01:01.287 --> 00:01:04.887
Then comes mitosis, when the cell divides into two new cells.

17
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:09.300
In interphase the cell is busy, not resting.

18
00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:14.780
It grows bigger. And it copies every one of its chromosomes.

19
00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:18.920
Each thread gets a matching copy, joined to it.

20
00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.040
Both stay thin and spread out for now.

21
00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:24.190
Look at the counter.

22
00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:27.990
Before copying, the cell had four chromosomes.

23
00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:31.510
Now each one is a pair of joined copies.

24
00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:35.090
Does the cell have four chromosomes, or eight?

25
00:01:41.533 --> 00:01:46.033
Four. A copied chromosome is still one chromosome.

26
00:01:47.313 --> 00:01:50.553
It is made of two identical copies, joined together.

27
00:01:51.813 --> 00:01:54.633
So the cell has four chromosomes, and eight copies.

28
00:01:55.683 --> 00:01:57.263
Now mitosis begins.

29
00:01:58.743 --> 00:02:01.223
Its four stages always come in the same order.

30
00:02:02.503 --> 00:02:04.803
In prophase, the chromosomes coil up.

31
00:02:06.043 --> 00:02:08.763
Each pair of copies becomes a thick X you can see.

32
00:02:09.767 --> 00:02:14.267
In metaphase, the chromosomes line up in a row across the middle of the cell.

33
00:02:15.627 --> 00:02:17.967
Red, blue, green and yellow, side by side.

34
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.840
In anaphase, the two copies of each chromosome are pulled apart.

35
00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:26.980
One copy of each colour moves to each end of the cell.

36
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.500
In telophase, two new nuclei form.

37
00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:33.220
Then the cell splits into two.

38
00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:36.440
Each new cell is called a daughter cell.

39
00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:39.150
Now count each daughter cell.

40
00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:41.770
Red, blue, green, yellow.

41
00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:44.730
Four chromosomes, one of each.

42
00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:47.990
That is the same set as the parent cell.

43
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:51.380
So here is the answer to our question.

44
00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:53.920
Copy first, then share.

45
00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:57.800
Copying makes two joined copies of every chromosome.

46
00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:01.840
Sharing sends one copy of each to each new cell.

47
00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:04.160
Nothing is missing.

48
00:03:05.233 --> 00:03:09.193
This is how body cells divide when you grow or when a scrape heals.

49
00:03:10.633 --> 00:03:11.633
Mistakes can happen.

50
00:03:12.413 --> 00:03:15.693
But normal division gives each new cell a full set.
